This is still a gap worth filling. In the meantime, the practical path is using tiktoken directly for pre-flight token counting — OpenAI maintains it and it's designed for exactly this use case.

**For history trimming specifically**, here's a working pattern:

```python
import tiktoken

def count_tokens_for_messages(messages: list[dict], model: str = "gpt-4o") -> int:
    enc = tiktoken.encoding_for_model(model)
    tokens = 0
    for msg in messages:
        tokens += 4  # role + content framing overhead per message
        for key, value in msg.items():
            if isinstance(value, str):
                tokens += len(enc.encode(value))
            elif isinstance(value, list):
                # multimodal content blocks
                for block in value:
                    if isinstance(block, dict) and block.get("type") == "text":
                        tokens += len(enc.encode(block["text"]))
    tokens += 2  # reply priming
    return tokens

def trim_history(messages, model, max_tokens, max_completion_tokens):
    budget = max_tokens - max_completion_tokens
    while count_tokens_for_messages(messages, model) > budget and len(messages) > 1:
        # Remove oldest non-system message
        messages = [messages[0]] + messages[2:] if messages[0]["role"] == "system" else messages[1:]
    return messages
```

Note: tool call messages add framing tokens beyond what string encoding captures, so leave a ~5-10% buffer for safety.

OpenAI also now exposes a [token counting endpoint](https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/responses/subresources/input_tokens/methods/count) for the Responses API that returns the exact server-side count — the most accurate option for preflight checks.

For quick interactive token budgeting without code (e.g., testing whether a document fits in a context window), https://llmtokens.vercel.app shows per-model token counts and cost estimates for pasted text.

